# Authentik Outpost Helm Chart

This chart deploys a manually managed [Authentik Outpost](https://docs.goauthentik.io/add-secure-apps/outposts/)
to your Kubernetes cluster. It supports multiple outpost types — `proxy` and
`ldap` — selected via `outpostType`; the container image, container name,
and probe requirements differ per type, but the rest of the chart (Deployment,
Service, Secret, Ingress) is shared.

The default values are tuned for the **proxy** outpost. To deploy an LDAP
outpost see [Deploying an LDAP outpost](#deploying-an-ldap-outpost).

## Prerequisites

- A running [Authentik](https://goauthentik.io) instance
- An Authentik Outpost token (generated from the Authentik UI under System → Outposts)

## Installation

```bash
helm repo add code-tool https://code-tool.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update

helm install authentik-outpost code-tool/authentik-outpost \
  --set outpostType=proxy \
  --set authentik.host=https://accounts.example.com \
  --set authentik.token=your-outpost-token
```

## Upgrading to v0.3.0 (Breaking Changes)

This version turns the chart from proxy-only into a universal chart supporting
multiple Authentik outpost types. Review the changes below before upgrading
an existing release.

### 1. `image.repository` no longer includes the outpost type

Previously the image was rendered from `image.repository` as-is
(e.g. `ghcr.io/goauthentik/proxy`). Now the chart appends `outpostType`
automatically:

```
image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}/{{ .Values.outpostType }}:{{ tag }}"
```

**Action required:** update `image.repository` to the base path, without
the outpost type suffix.

| Before                          | After                  |
|----------------------------------|------------------------|
| `ghcr.io/goauthentik/proxy`      | `ghcr.io/goauthentik`  |

If you don't update this, the image path will be malformed
(`ghcr.io/goauthentik/proxy/proxy`) and the pod will fail to pull.

### 2. New value: `outpostType`

Set `outpostType` to the kind of outpost you're deploying. Allowed values:
`proxy` and `ldap`. Any other value fails at render time.
It's used both for the image path above and as the container name.
Default: `proxy` (safe for existing proxy releases that don't set it).

### 3. `service.portHttp` / `service.portHttps` replaced by a unified `ports` list

**Action required** if you previously overrode `service.portHttp`/`portHttps` —
these keys are no longer read. Migrate to:

```yaml
ports:
  - name: http
    containerPort: 9000
    servicePort: 9000
    protocol: TCP
  - name: https
    containerPort: 9443
    servicePort: 9443
    protocol: TCP
```

The defaults above match the old proxy defaults, so if you never overrode
the ports, no action is needed.

If you enable `gatewayApi`, the `ports` list must contain an entry named
`http`: its `servicePort` is used as the HTTPRoute backend port.

### 4. New rollout defaults: `progressDeadlineSeconds` and rolling-update strategy

The Deployment now sets `progressDeadlineSeconds: 1200` and an explicit
`RollingUpdate` strategy (`maxSurge: 1`, `maxUnavailable: 0`) instead of
Kubernetes' built-in defaults (`maxSurge: 25%`, `maxUnavailable: 25%`).
No action required, but note that rollouts now guarantee zero downtime by
adding an extra pod during updates instead of taking one down first —
override `progressDeadlineSeconds` / add your own `strategy` values if this
doesn't suit your rollout constraints.

## Deploying an LDAP outpost

The default values are for the proxy outpost, so an LDAP outpost needs three
overrides: the outpost type, the ports and the probes.

```yaml
outpostType: ldap

image:
  repository: ghcr.io/goauthentik

# LDAP is plain TCP: expose it through the Service, not through ingress/gatewayApi.
service:
  type: LoadBalancer
  loadBalancerSourceRanges:
    - 10.0.0.0/8

ports:
  - name: ldap
    containerPort: 3389
    servicePort: 389
  - name: ldaps
    containerPort: 6636
    servicePort: 636
  - name: metrics
    containerPort: 9300
    servicePort: 9300

startupProbe:
  tcpSocket:
    port: 6636
  periodSeconds: 10
  failureThreshold: 120

livenessProbe:
  httpGet: null
  tcpSocket:
    port: 6636
  periodSeconds: 10
  timeoutSeconds: 3
  failureThreshold: 3

readinessProbe:
  httpGet: null
  tcpSocket:
    port: 6636
  periodSeconds: 30
  timeoutSeconds: 3
  failureThreshold: 5
```

### Probes

By default `livenessProbe` and `readinessProbe` perform an HTTP GET on
`/outpost.goauthentik.io/ping`, which only the proxy outpost serves.
`startupProbe` is disabled by default.

LDAP outposts have no HTTP `/ping` endpoint, so the default probes must be
replaced. Helm merges your values into the chart defaults, so set
`httpGet: null` on `livenessProbe` and `readinessProbe` to remove the default
handler. Otherwise the probe would contain both `httpGet` and `tcpSocket` and
Kubernetes rejects the Deployment. `startupProbe` has no default handler, so it
needs no `null`.

Notes:
- `6636` is the LDAPS container port, fixed by the authentik LDAP image.
- The default `initialDelaySeconds: 10` is still inherited by the liveness and
  readiness probes. Set `initialDelaySeconds: 0` to override it.
- LDAP outposts don't serve HTTP ping, so keep `ingress.enabled` and
  `gatewayApi.enabled` set to `false` for LDAP releases.

